1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,157
This is Danom, a solo project built for Monad Metropolis. It gives AI agents credit they can only spend where a sponsor allows.

2
00:00:09,157 --> 00:00:25,320
AI agents are starting to buy things on their own: API calls, data, compute. To let one do that today, you hand it a funded wallet. One leaked key, or one injected prompt, and the money is gone. So nobody extends credit to an agent.

3
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:43,874
Danom changes who holds the money. A lender vault pays the vendor, through x402 payment channels on Monad. The agent holds a signing key and nothing else. A sponsor sets the limits: which vendors, how much per channel, a ceiling, and an expiry. The contract enforces them.

4
00:00:43,874 --> 00:01:04,867
It is live on Monad testnet. A model driven agent buys data over HTTP four oh two. Monad's x402 facilitator verifies each voucher, and the vendor is paid from vault escrow. The agent's private key is published on the site. Even with it, the money cannot go anywhere the sponsor did not approve.

5
00:01:04,867 --> 00:01:19,806
Why build this? Because an agent that repays builds a record on chain, and that record follows it to any sponsor. As it grows, the same collateral supports more credit. That is how an agent earns trust it did not start with.

6
00:01:19,806 --> 00:01:34,431
This is a testnet preview, and the open problems are stated plainly in the repository, starting with how to price that risk. But the limits are real, and you can test them yourself. Danom. Credit for agents, with limits you can check.

